    "result": "I implemented `check_password` in `solution.py` and wrote 29 pytest tests in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` passes. The implementation follows the spec: a `TypeError` for non-`str` arguments, then the five rules checked in the order LENGTH, CLASSES, USERNAME, REPEAT, BLOCKLIST. I added two small private helpers, `_has_three_classes` and `_has_triple_repeat`, and kept the function signature unchanged. The tests cover each rule's boundaries (length 12 and 64, username of 2 and 3 characters, two versus three repeats), the case-sensitivity cases, the substring-versus-equality blocklist case, and the output order. I did not run the coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit gates, so those are unchecked. The one thing I'd expect to matter is the mutation gate, since some mutants (for example `<` to `<=` on the length bounds) are only caught by the boundary tests, which are present.",
